g about what we  thought would be something useful  whereas with the wrangling one we did  that late where we assembled an entire  taxonomy with the truly brought them up  process  and then thought about could we come up  with something different that would  actually give us generative power so it  was a late stage reflective synthesis  in terms of you know connecting with  previous research literature we  basically just mined that for some  source material for the timelines  project  we didn't explicitly do a viz research  search although we were certainly  informed by that as part of the genetic  project and then we very explicitly  tried to make sure we connected to that  research what i'll call a light mapping  pass with the wrangling project and a  really deep and thorough mapping path to  that previous work in the task project  so thinking about descriptive power um  we tried validation against a test set  for the timeline project we had this  really systematic method um that gave us  a lot of very comprehensive coverage for  the genepi project  we had this really high precision  descriptive power that allowed us to  find  where the other work that wasn't  specific to journalists diverged from  what that group did  and then we had this um quite widespread  adoption of the task taxonomy  showing that there was quite a bit of  descriptive power in that one  now for generative power for two of  these we built one we built in the paper  itself one we built in in implementation  in a follow-up paper uh the third we  plan to build um so this idea that you  could use a design space as the basis  for some sort of software implementation  um and then there was quite a bit of  usage of the abstract cast technology  when people were doing these sort of um  design focused visualization projects  after that was published  now what i think is interesting is  thinking about the descriptive versus  the generative power  so in one in the timelines we had  something where we used analysis to  characterize a subset uh to give us more  generative power and with gen epi we use  the exactly the same ones we didn't have  this distinction and it was a very  detailed fine-grained one  whereas with the wrangling paper we  didn't take a subset we actually  developed something completely new to  get generative power  and then with the abstract tasks again  it was the same but it was a very  concise one so we had generative power  in mind from the beginning as we thought  about those so these different um  approaches and we really focused much  more on evaluative power really only in  that last one  and that brings me to discussing the  assessment what i find is i always think  about generative and descriptive power  and i find sometimes it's less easy to  think about when could you distinguish  the power to evaluate alternatives from  the power to generate new or describe  existing and i find often in my own  thinking that's less obviously a third  category than the descriptive versus  generative  so i want to come back as a wrap up to  this thinking about well how do we  assess taxonomies and to sort of frame  this in the larger context of different  kinds of theories  so for example ben schneiderman who's  designing the ui book talks about  descriptive theories explanatory  theories you can imagine taxonomies  possibly fitting into that but  definitely this idea of a prescriptive  theory or predictive theory that's not  what a taxonomy or a design space is  trying to do  and i really like the way that paul  ralph talked about it in this really  intriguing paper that i'm going to  encourage folks in hci and viz to really  dive into it's quite recent  and um he talks about different kinds of  theories as theories for understanding  where you organize something into useful  categories that's exactly a taxonomy  versus process theories where you think  about how something happens uh ralph  argues these are often sort of augmented  taxonomies so it's 