  um one of the downsides of virtual talks  is that you can't hear all the clapping  so uh we have plenty of time for  questions um and so we'll open up the  floor for those uh if you're an attendee  please  post your question to the to the  chat  and  will will help us get those  uh to  tomorrow um  so  while those are coming in  um  i had a question which is  okay that was a really great um  you know it's really great to see those  three different examples of how to build  this kind of taxonomy and design space  and  one of the things that i was wondering  is um  have you thought about tools that could  help you  build these kinds of taxonomies for  different design spaces  um  at least somewhat  so for example that  that  gevit project where we had this idea of  you need tools that lets you go inhale a  giant corpus of literature and help you  find these topics so you could do the  sampling um anna actually ended up just  instantiating that as an r package  called adjutant we have a separate paper  that's just introducing that and people  have ended up using that for this kind  of uh taxonomy creation where you want  to sample a really large literature so  we have at least um got one tool for  that i think there's so much more that  you could do um and i think particularly  i mean i know there's a lot of software  for sort of doing generic open coding  but in a lot of the ways that we did our  open coding i don't know that it would  have helped i think that if your source  material is like  audio files where you want to create  transcripts and then code them or video  files i think there's some software  support for that but where you're  thinking about you know the space of  timelines you know matt brainer did a  whole lot with pencil and paper um and  pen and paper so i i feel like the more  exotic your source material the more we  don't have good tools and one of the  things i emphasize in this talk was just  how broad your set of source material  could be it'