uld talk a  little bit about how you choose the  domains  to focus on what's do you have a process  there  how does that how does that work for you  um you mean like how we pick to do these  different projects like gen epi versus  journalism yeah  the whole project at all yeah no just  how yeah versus timelines versus tasks  yeah um  i mean you know the short answer is i  tend to be pretty opportunistic and you  know um anna came and was someone who  was an expert in genomic epidemiology  and wanted to do a phd and you know  going deeper into visualization for gen  epi so that's how we picked the domain  because that was her domain of expertise  and interest  um you know with timelines uh that was  something when matt bremer was at  microsoft research  uh on an internship during his phd it  was a bit of a continuation of some work  we had done with a project called  timeline curator which was an authoring  system for timeline so we'd we'd sort of  already started thinking about temporal  data and then he just went to town and  went deeper into that  with the data wrangling for journalists  steve kasich came to me as someone who  had a background in journalism and  decided visualization was great and  backfilled himself into a computer  science background just so we could  visualize stuff in a journalistic  context so we sort of he came to me with  that domain in mind already  and then it took a bunch of looking  around until we decided to focus on  wrangling in particular is a nice open  area so you know i feel like my answer  is  depending on who comes to me in terms of  collaborators or um you know the  possibility of people with data sets and  so on i tend to just say  let's see if there's anything here that  could make a cool research project so  that's not a very principled answer but  that's the reality well that's fine are  there areas that you think would be ripe  for  you know you or others to to look at  um  guess i tend to think that  i mean i personally 